>the legend goes.

I think I read this in Gordon Bell's book on the history of DEC.

I believe that the problem was that KO could not get capitalization to make
computers (too risky to compete with Univac and IBM), so DEC was started as a
module manufacturer. Since the loan agreement prohibited making computers, Ken
built Programmeble Data Processors instead. The 18 bit PDP-1 was fairly
successful and the rest ...

In any case, the government bought more computers from Univac than from IBM
in those days.

