jmgallau@mailbox.syr.edu (John Gallaugher) wrote:
>I'm trying to upgrade an old DEC 433 pc in our lab.  It's a flat unit,
>about as tall as a single full height drive, with a 3.5" disk drive on the
>right hand side & the power switch on the left.  The boot drive on this
>unit comes from an external SCSI box with a case profile similar to the
>PC's.  The box has a 200MB hard drive (RZ24) in it.
>
>I'm trying to add a 1 GB SCSI drive & CD ROM.  I've removed the
>terminators from these devices since the SCSI box is externally
>terminated.  When I reboot the machine, the new devices power up, but are
>NOT recognized at all.  In trying to deduce the problem I removed the
>external terminator from the SCSI box & booted, thinking that if the
>machine booted, then the boot drive must be internally terminated and the
>external terminator wasn't really necessary.  The machine booted when I
>removed the external termiantor, so I assume that means the boot drive is
>internally terminated.  Is this a correct assumption?
<snip>

From SCSI 101:

1st.  You must make sure that the is termination only at the end of the SCSI bus
      Multiple terminations will mess it up. (so check the RZ24)

2nd.  Assuming you are using DOS/Wind, make sure that the new DISK is at SCSI ID#1
      This is the only other ID used by a disk without an extra scsi driver.

3rd.  Put the CD-ROM at ID 4.  (Either 6 or 7 is the controller!)

4th   Make sure the new drive is set to 'spin up on power up'.  Usually a jumper
      on the drive.

Hope this helps...

Jeff




