From: mew@world.std.com (Michael E Willett)
Subject: New RPMMark benchmark results: AS/400 versus Alpha
Date: 1995/10/29
Message-ID: <DH7vs4.CzH@world.std.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 118278933
organization: The World Public Access UNIX, Brookline, MA
newsgroups: comp.benchmarks,comp.sys.dec

The October 27 issue of Midrange Systems has a big article on
the results of the new IBM RPMark95 benchmarks run by Client/Server
Labs in Atlanta.  The first results are a vendor-commissioned
study on the impact of adding a second processor to three different
servers, including a Compaq ProLiant 2000, DEC Alpha 4/275
and an AS/400 Model 30S.  The dual-IMPI processing AS/400 posted the
highest RPMark95 rating while the DEC Alpha offered the best price/
performance.  The AS/400 also offered the best scaling of overall
performance with the addition of a second processor.

The RPMark measures the overall performance of a client/server
configuration based on three concurrently running workloads:
Decision Support, OLTP, and File/Serving/Office Automation.
The RPMark was originally developed by IBM, and is now 
independently managed by Client/Server Labs.

