In article <1995Oct27.172534.11679@eisner>,
Harri Kylliainen <kylliainen@eisner.decus.org> wrote:
>Last DEC User-magazine's editorial included interesting rumour: there is a
>possibility to get Compaq with Alpha-processor. Has anybody more information
>about this ?
>

It remains a rumour at this point. And a potential convergence of some 
strange bedfellows indeed: DEC regards Compaq as the Main Enemy in the 
low-end server space, and Compaq has developed a sudden attraction for 
networking capabilities.

This being said, it's no secret that Compaq is no friend of Intel. Could
it be that the folks in Austin see some merit in the Alpha value
proposition that Bob Palmer preaches: "Alpha is at least twice as fast as 
Intel and will remain so. Competitive RISC microprocessors that are 
different from Intel but only marginally faster just won't cut it"?

And it's also no secret that Compaq forged a service alliance with 
Digital. Could it be that still waters run deep?

During a recent briefing from Digital Semiconductor, an Alpha marketeer 
stated that DEC had no near-term plans to pitch Alpha to first-tier PC 
vendors. Naturally, I brought up the Compaq rumour. "Compaq is a special 
case," replied the marketeer.

And at least one very prominent DEC executive responded to the same 
rumour with "well, they [Compaq} are talking about it."

Stay tuned.


