In Article <46rd2l$t86@usenetp1.news.prodigy.com>
RBQD60A@prodigy.com (Ashit Kapadia) writes:

>After years of using a UNIX account my account was replaced with a VAX 
>mainframe account. Could some kindly soul provide me with the basics of 
>VAX. How to use gopher, ftp, usenet access, telnet etc. if there is a FAQ 
>somewhere then please point me towards it.  Also unix has tons of 
>newsgroups, but a search for DEC and VAX revealed very few newsgroups. 
>What gves???

There's only one flavor of VMS, so there are not lots of different newsgroups.
Be sure to look at the vmsnet newsgroups as they are VERY VMS oriented.

You use things like gopher, telnet, ftp, and the like just like you do on a
Unix system. There are several different newsreaders for VMS, but none is a
direct port of one of the popular Unix ones. You'll need to talk to the people
who run the system to get details.

The VMS FAQ is posted monthly to comp.sys.dec, comp.os.vms, vmsnet.alpha, and
probably some others. I thing it came out about a week ago, so it may be a
while.

Finally, the thing that is most important in learning top get around VMS is
HELP. Most system programs support a HELP command and HELP at the main (DCL)
prompt will provide information on all system commands.

Finally, if you really want to feel at home, try the Posix shell. It gives all
of the Posix standard Unix commands like ls, rm, mv, tar, vi yacc, etc. (To
find out about Posix, use HELP POSIX) Posix is an optional installation, so it
is possible that it has not been installed on your system. Also Internet
support is optional. It may be you don't have FTP, telnet, and so on. It's
available, but installation and licenseing is up to the site.

