In article <1995Oct18.165656.26610@nosc.mil>, blombard@nosc.mil (Bob Lombard) writes:
> I'm inheriting admin responsibility for an 11/750 with RA-81's 
> and a TU-77 tape.  The system is running BSD 4.2
> 
> The TU-77 is dead and not repairable.  Any suggestions for a Tape 
> drive I could replace the TU-77 with ?  Hopefully a plug-in and 
> run solution..

  Why do you say it's not repairable? The TU77/78/79 drives are wonderful
units, as long as you keep Field Service away from them. What exactly is it
doing wrong? If you want, follow up via email and I'll talk you through
fixing it.

  Any other Massbus tape drive with a TM02/TM03 formatter would be a drop-
in replacement. A TU78/79 (with a TM78 formatter) would need a new driver,
which might not be available in 4.2BSD (I think it was - I know it's in
4.3BSD).

  If you're going for reliability and don't need the 800BPI capability of
the TU77 (note - the TU78 is 1600/6250 - no 800 there either), you could
get a TU80. This uses a single quad-size Unibus card you plug in the 750 and
2 50-pin ribbon cables to a small drive (the size of the 750's cabinet with-
out the extra width). This would also need a new kernel, but support for the
TU80 is in 4.2BSD.

