>I'm trying to upgrade an old DEC 433 pc in our lab.  It's a flat unit,

It's called a pizza box format.
 
>I'm trying to add a 1 GB SCSI drive & CD ROM.  I've removed the
>terminators from these devices since the SCSI box is externally
>terminated.  When I reboot the machine, the new devices power up, but are
>NOT recognized at all.  In trying to deduce the problem I removed the
>external terminator from the SCSI box & booted, thinking that if the

The terminators were correct.  Nothing should be terminated inside the
box.
 
>Also: is there some kind of SCSI probe I can use with the built in
>controller for the 433 unit?  If so, where can I download it from & what
>is it called?  I suppose I will need drivers for the CD ROM drive &
>perhaps the hard disk, too.  Is this correct?  If so, where can I find
>drivers compatible with this internal/external SCSI mechanism?
> 
>Thanks GREATLY for any advice folks might be able to offer!


There is very little auto-probing that goes on under DOS.  The Adaptec
controller in the DECpc 433 will see and report the devices found on 
the bus, but that scrolls by awfully fast during bootup.  You might
want to add a /v switch to the scsi driver in the config.sys file to 
see more.

Another thing.  This is DOS, just because a disk is installed doesn't
mean DOS uses it.  You must run the afdisk.exe command to create a 
DOS partition before it will acknowledge the disk and give you access
to it.

The CDROM won't become available until you follow the instructions
for installing the CDROM driver and the MSCDEX driver.  These usually
come with the CDROM drive.  If not, you'll have to see if the DEC
manuals tell you how.  If you can't find those, I'll see if I can 
dig mine out and give you a hand.


