Eric,

In article <468f3s$ivf@lace.Colorado.EDU> efricha@tigger.cs.Colorado.EDU (Eric F. Richards) wrote:
> We'd like to replace two of the existing 4M simms with a pair of 32M
> simms.
> 	1) Does it matter which pair I replace?  A quick try to replace
> 	   the "lower" simms was unsuccessful.  (I'm assuming that
> 	   the simms can be replaced in sets of two -- is that
> 	   true?)

The bad news is the DEC2000 model 300 takes SIMMs in two sets of 4 and
yes, it does matter the order in which SIMMs are used. There are two 
alternating memory banks of 4 SIMMs each. I can't find my ASCII drawing
of the motherboard so this will have to do:

	edge of board
Key/Mou	Bank 0 SIMM 0		CPU
	Bank 1 SIMM 0
serial	Bank 0 SIMM 1
	Bank 1 SIMM 1
	Bank 0 SIMM 2
||el	Bank 1 SIMM 2
	Bank 0 SIMM 3
	Bank 1 SIMM 3

Bank 0 must always be filled with SIMMs, using Bank 1 is optional. All
SIMMs in a bank must be identical, and each bank must be fully populated
to work.
 
> 	2) will I need to play with the eisa config floppy before I
> 	   put the simms in?  After?

No.

> 	3) Are there jumpers to be set on that board when changing
> 	   memory, or does it autoconfig?

No, the firmware detects the size of memory installed in each bank and
configures accordingly. If the memory fails then the system reports the
failure on the LEDs near the parallel port. 'On off off on' indicates that
there was a memory failure in Bank 0, 'On off on off' indicates the same 
for Bank 1.
 
> 	4) Is there freely available (or really cheap) documentation
> 	   around somewhere on this system that I could obtain?

I have a book called "Customer Technical Information" for this system 
which explain the above. The Part Number on the cover is EK-A0636-TM.001


Gordon


