>'disc not High Sierra or ISO9660 format, abort/retry ...'
>
>Booting FreeBSD on the PC, can 'mount -t cd9660 /dev/cd0c /mnt' no probs and do an 'ls -lR"
>on the Windows hierarchy.
>
>Everything looks fine at the DOS level. I'm wondering if I'm seeing the reverse of the above AXP 
>turbochannel problem.
>
>The RRD42 is in an external case, there are no visible switches/jumpers.
>If the RRD42 is the cause of the problems, I can try to grab a more regular SCSI cdrom from a PC.
>
>Appreciate any help.
>
>tony

I don't know whether or not this is the cause of your problem, but 
RRD42s do indeed have dual personalities.  The RRD42s that I have seen 
are actually SONY CDU-541s.  (I think that the RRD43 or RRD44 is the 
Toshiba drive.)

The back of the SONY 541 has some jumper pins for SCSI ID, parity, etc. 
One of these is labelled TEST.  If the RRD42 firmware is installed, the 
drive is an RRD42 when the TEST jumper is installed.  It is a PC/Mac 
CDROM when the jumper is removed.

DISCLAIMER:  This has been my experience with these drives.  I have no 
idea whether or not it is true for all of them.

