In article <4673m8$de3@odin.sunquest.com>, tony@sunquest.com (Tony Jones) writes:
> The RRD42 is in an external case, there are no visible switches/jumpers.
> If the RRD42 is the cause of the problems, I can try to grab a more regular SCSI cdrom from a PC.
> 
> Appreciate any help.

  An RRD42 is a Sony CDU-541 with DEC-specific firmware. On a real 541, there
is a jumper to enable/disable SCSI parity. On the DEC variant, this jumper
sets the sector size (512 for DEC, 2048 for industry-standard) and the vendor
ID returned by the drive. Usually there is a short purple wire loop on the con-
nector which you can cut or carefully remove. (This is the connector that con-
nects the external ID switch to the drive).

  The Sony CDU-561 seems to have some intelligence in it for dealing with 
DEC systems - it appears to automatically determine the sector size the host
wants, so it will work on both "normal" and DEC systems without any changes.

