>   {
>      printf ("Please enter float number: ");
>      if (scanf  ("%f", &f) == 1)
>      {
>         cptr = (char*)&f;
>         printf ("Byte 0: %4d, Byte 1: %4d, Byte 2: %4d, Byte 4: %4d\n",
>                 (int)cptr[0], (int)cptr[1], (int)cptr[2], (int)cptr[3]);
>      }
>      else
>         abbruch = 1;
>
>   } while (!abbruch);
>
>   return (0);
>}
>------------------8<------------------8<------------------8<---------

Classical programming error!  Replace "char" by "unsigned char" in your
program and you'll get the same results, except for the byte order, of
course, on both SGI and Alpha.

On SGI, char is unsigned, while on Alpha, char is signed (the "signedness"
of char is undefined by the language).  

All the workstations (I know of) use IEEE-754 floating point.  Only the
byte sex is different between little endian and big endian platforms.

BTW, you don't have to cast the char arguments of printf.  The compiler
will do it for you, because printf is a variadic function and the 
default argument promotions are performed automatically on all the
arguments following the format string.

Dan
