rossgod@eowyn.iwb.mw.tu-muenchen.de (Ulrich Rossgoderer) writes:

|>I've to read a binary file generated on a SGI or a HP workstation. The
|>binary contains float numbers (single precision)?
|>
|>How do I read (convert) them on a DEC-ALPHA workstation? It can't be
|>only done by byte swapping, because the following program
[snip]
|>
|>produces the following output on a SGI:
|>---------------------------------------
|>
|>Please enter float number: 1.0
|>Byte 0:   63, Byte 1:  128, Byte 2:    0, Byte 4:    0
|>
|>Please enter float number: -1.0
|>Byte 0:  191, Byte 1:  128, Byte 2:    0, Byte 4:    0
|>
|>and on a DEC-ALPHA:
|>-------------------
|>
|>Please enter float number: 1.0
|>Byte 0:    0, Byte 1:    0, Byte 2: -128, Byte 4:   63
|>
|>Please enter float number: -1.0
|>Byte 0:    0, Byte 1:    0, Byte 2: -128, Byte 4:  -65

If you look closer, you'd see that the Alpha values really are the "same"
(except for byte ordering) as the SGI - on SGI, you're getting the values
displayed as unsigned byte integers, whereas on Alpha they're signed bytes.
(%d is supposed to be signed decimal, so I think the SGI results are wrong.)
The bit patterns are the same (try using %x instead).
