I'm not sure this is your real problem but it may point you in the right 
direction.  The /sbin/swapdefault is a symbolic link to the primary swap 
device when you want to reserve swap space as programs start (i.e. if 
swap less than physical memory you will be denied process forks if you 
run out of swap).  If this file is removed you receive the bootup message 
you reported and swap space is not reserved until physical memory is 
exhausted (SCO like swap).

If you are receiving this message it probably means that the device which 
is your primary swap partition is no longer startable.  If this is on 
your root drive you may be having a drive failure.

One thing you could try is boot your install CD and choose Advanced 
install.  You should be allowed to exit at some point and mount your hard 
drives.  NOTE: you will need to create the device nodes using 
/dev/MAKEDEV so you will have to know the rz# of your drives.

