tigger@solaria.sol.net (Tim Thompson) writes:


>I've recently acquired an 11/44, and am going to attempt
>to get it functioning, and installed in my rack.  I'll be

Good. The 11/44 is quite a nice machine - one of the nicer 22-bit addressing
Unibus machines for home use. There's almost nothing custom in the CPU, 
and it's small enough to fit in the room :-). 

>testing it within the next day or two, and if it proves to
>be working, I wouldn't mind to get ahold of the following

If it isn't working, post here, and I'm sure somebody will talk you through
repairing it. You may want to read the 'how to power up an old machine 
for the first time' file if you haven't already.

>items and information:

>1.)  An expansion backplane.
>     (It is currently in a BA11-A box, with a 7410 power supply.
>      There is currently a 14-slot backplane, and I'd like to add
>      at least 9 slots more.  The backplane I need appears to have
>      two flat copper power conductors going to the power supply,
>      and a 3rd flat conductor with a berg header on the end of it.)

That's how the CPU backplane is connected to the PSU, sure, but normally 
there's a distribution panel on the PSU which provides 3 15 pin power 
connectors and 3 6 pin control connectors for normal backplanes. Check to 
see if you have this - if not, you need that first. Then you can use any 
standard 9-slot expansion backplane, like a DD11-D. I'd be suprised if 
this distribution panel was missing, unless parts have been removed from 
your system.

[...]

>Information:

>I'm assuming this unit does 22-bit addressing,
>so, hopefully, I can stick 4MB of memory into it.

Yes. There's a 22 bit MMU in the CPU board set, along with the Unibus 
Mapping Registers to allow DMA to access all of memory. There's only 18 
bit addressing on the unibus, of course.

>Are any of the slots in the backplane reserved,
>or can I stick the processor set in the first 5 slots?
>(Currently, the console interface is in slot 1, and the
> 11/44 starts in slot 4.)

They're virtually all reserved. From memory, the 14 slots of the CPU 
backplane must be filled as follows : 

1) A,B M7090 console interface, C-F M7091 CIS (option)
2) M7092 CIS (option)
3) M7093 FPU (option)
4) M7094 Data Paths (ALU + registers)
5) M7095 CPU control (Microcode + sequencer)
6) M7096 MFM board (MultiFunction Module - Console processor, etc)
7) M7097 Cache memory (option?)
8) M7098 Unibus Interface (includes Unibus Map)
9-12) Memory slots. IIRC, you don't put grant cards in empty memory slots
13) Hex-height SPC slot. Put a grant card in here if it's not used
14) A,B Unibus Out (or M9302 terminator), C-F Quad height SPC slot (put
    a grant card in here if it's not used) 

>Where can boards go? (Assuming the proper grant cards are installed.)

Hopefully the above will help you.

>Can I put Quad boards in, starting in C/D, so that they
>extend into E/F?  Can Dual boards go into E/F?

In theory, yes, but only if they're the right boards. Quad-height SPC's 
(Small Peripheral Controllers) always go in C-F of a slot - you can put 
those in slot 13 or 14 of the CPU backplane, as above. Very old SPC cards 
(as in early 70's - am I the only person still seriously using them ?) 
are dual height, and go in C,D. You then put an M105 address selector in 
E, and an M782 (or M7820, M7821) interrupt controller in F. Some 3rd 
party boards integrated the latter 2 functions onto a second dual card 
that you put in E,F. 

>When do I need to install/remove the nifty little jumper wires
>(tagged with red) on the backplane?

The only jumpers you ever need to touch are the NPG (Non-Processor Grant 
- a DMA grant) links between pins CA1 and CB1 of an SPC slot. They must 
be removed if there's an DMA device, such as a disk controller, in the 
slot. They must be fitted if the device does not make any connections to 
these pins.

Here's a handy way of finding out. Look at the top 2 edge pins on 
connector C of the card you wish to install, on the component side. Now :

If they are connected into the logic, you have a DMA device -> Remove the 
link

If they are jumpered together (e.g. DZ-11) -> It doesn't matter - the 
jumper is in parallel with the link

If they are unconnected -> fit the link


>And, lastly (out of curiousity) what clock speed is the thing using?
>(I glanced at the boards once, and noticed a 5.xxxx Mhz TTL Clock/Osc,
>but didn't think that was it. . .)

The 5.xx MHz crystal is for the baud rate generators for the console and 
TU58 ports. I forget what the CPU clock is, but it's obtained from a 
delay line on one of the CPU boards - I can look up what the clock 
actually is

>Tim

-tony




