Tim Thompson <tigger@solaria.sol.net> wrote:
>
>I've recently acquired an 11/44, and am going to attempt
>to get it functioning, and installed in my rack.  I'll be
>testing it within the next day or two, and if it proves to
>be working, I wouldn't mind to get ahold of the following
>items and information:
>
>1.)  An expansion backplane.
>     (It is currently in a BA11-A box, with a 7410 power supply.
>      There is currently a 14-slot backplane, and I'd like to add
>      at least 9 slots more.  The backplane I need appears to have
>      two flat copper power conductors going to the power supply,
>      and a 3rd flat conductor with a berg header on the end of it.)
>

	You want a DD11-DK. Not *too* hard to find, but I tend to covet
the ones I run into :-)

>2.)  An M7093 11/44 Floating Point Unit.

	good luck. Probably harder to find than the dd11.

>
>
>3.)  Perhaps (eventually) some sort of Unibus SMD controller.

	UDA-50 I believe. I have one, and it works, but I'm using it, and
have no spare. :-( There are "aftermarket" controllers (Dilog, Emulex,
etc.) also. I don't know the model numbers. My 11/44 has a dual SCSI
interface from Emulex. Works great :-)

>
>
>
>Information:
>
>I'm assuming this unit does 22-bit addressing,
>so, hopefully, I can stick 4MB of memory into it.

	Yup, no problem. 11/44 does 16, 18, and 22 bit addressing. Also
has all three modes: kernel, user, and supervisor. Pretty cool.

>
>Are any of the slots in the backplane reserved,
>or can I stick the processor set in the first 5 slots?
>(Currently, the console interface is in slot 1, and the
> 11/44 starts in slot 4.)


	Whoa. You need a copy of the 11/44 processor manual. Don't go moving
the CPU boards around - they need to be in the correct slots. Working from
memory, no pun intended, or see a previous post on this subject ( Tony? ),
CIM is in slot 1 with nothing else (it's dual width), slot 2 gets CIS,
slot 3 gets FPU, and the next 6 slots or so are for processor boards.
Finally, there are four slots for memory, then two Unibus slots: one hex,
the other quad (to leave room for the expander to the aforementioned DD-11).

>
>Where can boards go? (Assuming the proper grant cards are installed.)
>Can I put Quad boards in, starting in C/D, so that they
>extend into E/F?  Can Dual boards go into E/F?

	Quad, definitely, NPR jumper is in row C, and I think the BG
lines are in row D. Not sure about dual-width: maybe they have to
go in C/D also?

>
>When do I need to install/remove the nifty little jumper wires
>(tagged with red) on the backplane?

	Hmmm. Mine are yellow :-| These are the NPR jumpers. If the board
in the slot does not use NPR, the jumper has to be in. Otherwise it has to
be out. Or you could do as I've done - take out all the jumpers, and solder
a bridge across the two appropriate board traces on all my unibus cards that
don't use NPR, and remember to use a NPR & grant card in all empty slots.
That way I can move cards from slot to slot more or less indiscriminantly, and
not have to mess with the backplane.

>
>And, lastly (out of curiousity) what clock speed is the thing using?
>(I glanced at the boards once, and noticed a 5.xxxx Mhz TTL Clock/Osc,
>but didn't think that was it. . .)

	Don't have the prints in front of me, but isn't the 11/44 CPU
asynchronous? There is probably an effective average clock rate,  and it's not
very fast :-)

