newsgroups: comp.sys.dec,alt.sys.pdp11

>Does anyone know why DEC didn't duplicate the registers on the 11/44 
>(they did on the 11/45) so that all ALU operations take at least 3 
>register cycles? This seems to be crazy to me - the extra logic needed to 
>switch register addresses round, provide latches in the other ALU leg, 
>offsets the saving by only having one set of register RAM. 

The 11/45 was a blazing machine for 1972, With bipolar memory (11/55) it 
could burst at 3 MIPS. The registers were duplicated to keep the 74S TTL 
within design specs. The fast edge speeds of 74S logic required 
transmission line design rules. By duplicating the regs and thus keeping 
the maximum length of any line to a minimum you did not have to worry as 
much about probigation deleys and race conditions in hardware. 

I use to keep an 11/45 in my garage with a full house of bipolar speed 
memory in it.

The edge speed in 74S logic is less than 2 nS as I recall thus making 
long traces an art... Later technologys, 74LS, had 5 to 6 nS edge speeds. 
Much easier to design with. Kinda like the MECL III vs 10K ECL families.

Regards, Ethan
