reply-to: muswick@max.uh.cwru.edu
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


Dear Jerry,

    I have a big advantage of where I work, we use a 1.5 Tesla MRI to
demagnetize our tapes.  We just walk in, move the tapes in and out of
the scanner bore a few times, and you have a blank tape.

    Now, since that probaly is not a great solution for most people,
what you may look to is either a tabletop demagnetizer $200-$300 and
available from broadcast suppliers or a hand-held unit, which about $40 to
$80 and available from fine audio/video stores.  (Radio Shack used to carry
them, you might try some, since some stores keep old stock for many years.)

   I used to be a broadcast engineer and my experience with the table top units
were favorable since you could leave the tapes on it for a while.  The 
hand-held units work best if you place the tape on a metal (iron) surface and 
use the demagnetizer over the tape.  This increases the effectiveness of the 
small units.  Another advice is to flip the tapes.

    The problem of initializing tapes is not restricted to DLT media.  I
had the same problems using 800 bpi tapes on 1600/800 bpi drive, and
1600 bpi tapes on a 1600/6250 drive.  The problem seems to be with any drive
that supports multiple densities.  Once the drive recognizes the media/format
on loading, the drive gets locked to that density and format.

Good luck.     

Gary Muswick
Image Processing Engineer.
Univ. Hospitals of Cleveland

