In article <45g3um$16s@irz401.inf.tu-dresden.de>,
Sebastian Schoenberg <ss10@irz.inf.tu-dresden.de> wrote:
>im working on a portation of an microkernel to the Alpha
>processor. Can anybody tell me, which papers or manuals are available
>from DEC about programming a new PALcode ? In specially the handling
>of interrupts, TB faults and bootstrapping mechanisms. Please give me
>hints about commercial books and DEC handouts.

The only (remotely) official way of getting this stuff at the time we
did it was to buy an evaluation board.  With such a board, one gets a
software developer's kit, which now comes on a CD (the March '95 one I
have, which I suspect is slightly out of date, is order AG-QBXSB-BE).

Actually, I would recommend an evaluation board anyway; writing
PALcode has a lot in common with any conceivable worst nightmare, and
it's nice to be in an environment where you're somewhat cushioned.

Then you need to get the manuals relating to the workstation you're
going to use; this is like extracting hen's teeth.  We were going to
use DEC 3000s, and there _is_ (or was) a Systems Programmers' Manual
for the whole series.  However, it didn't appear in the Digital Europe
price list, so any ordinary salesman would quite reasonably deny its
existence.  We eventually got a copy through Technical OEM Support
(we're not an OEM, of course).

All this ought to change if you're going for the right hardware.  If I
were starting today, I would specify the noName AXP/PCI board (or the
EB164 if I could summon the money to do the `right thing').  These
things are expected to run Linux, and therefore the documentation
ought to be available pretty widely.  If, however, you're stuck with
something that comes out of Digital Systems (the evaluation boards and
the SDK, not to mention the support of the Linux effort, are from
Digital Semiconductor).

Then there's boot loaders ... go for the Semiconductor one, since the
one that comes with systems is bigger than the Linux kernel (let alone
any "microkernel" ;-).

All in all, your best bet is to get in touch with a local
representative of Digital Semiconductor.  Good luck!
--
