In article <1995Oct10.170922.1@sldb6.slac.stanford.edu>, 
fairfield@sldb6.slac.stanford.edu writes:

|>        But since you've got  an  Alpha  with  DEC  Fortran, Digital has
|>    already  solved  your problem: you can use the non-standard  keyword
|>    CONVERT in the OPEN statement on the Alpha (both for read and write)
|>    to convert to and from big-endian IEEE format.  This will work  fine
|>    if  all  your  data  are  single-  or  double-precision  REALs.   My
|>    recollection is that it won't work  for mixed data type records (but
|>    check  me  on this).  However, on OpenVMS you can call the  Run-time
|>    Library  routine  CVT$CONVERT_FLOAT  to  do  the  conversion  on  an
|>    item-by-item basis.  I would guess, but I don't know, that a similar
|>    conversion function exists for DEC UNIX.

It does work for mixtures of datatypes in a record, but not if you use the
STRUCTURE/RECORD extension.  The CONVERT= option is available on all
DEC Fortran platforms (and will also do endian-swaps for integers.)

The Digital UNIX equivalent of CVT$CONVERT_FLOAT is cvt_ftof.
