In article <DGC6Cx.Bz6@world.std.com>, shannon@world.std.com (Terry C Shannon) writes:
> In article <45i80r$bsu@news.isc.rit.edu>,
> SIMMONS <dds3769@osfmail.isc.rit.edu> wrote:
>>What kind of a backplane is this? The three end slots I recognize
>>as QBUS but the rest I don't know what they are. And what was the
>>processor that this might have originally come with?
> 
> The VAXstation 3520 was a dual-processor VAX workstation that came out in 
> the late 1980's. It incorporated CMOS VAX microprocessors that delivered 
> around ~3 VUPS apiece. DEC also offered the VAXstation 3540, a 
> quad-processor version.

The rest of the slots are M-Bus (no, not the same as the SPARC one), a
proprietary write-back cache bus. The M-Bus is managed by the FBIC, the
big chip with a cooling tower at the top of the board near the fingers.

The processor module has two CVAXes on it; each CVAX has its own FBIC. There
are two options that convert to QBus: the FTAM, which supports only the
TK70 tape controller, and the FQAM, which is a more generic QBus adapter.

The CPU module is L2001. My memory module says L2007 and is mostly
unpopulated; I forget how much memory my system has. The I/O module includes
SCSI, Ethernet, and a few serial ports. The graphics option takes three
modules and is powered by its very own MicroVAX II CPU.

I haven't run my system in a few years, primarily because the cable between
the I/O module and the panel containing the thinwire and serial spigots is
flakey.
