In article <44ujve$2jq@newstand.syr.edu>, kbecker@npac.syr.edu (Kathy Becker) writes:
>I have 25 VXT 2000+ booting off an Infoserver 150. Intermittently,
>when one of the VXT 2000+ boots, the following message is displayed
>at the top of the VXT 2000+ screen:
>
>	Cannot extend Pagefile more disk space needed on server.
>
>This message will repeat itself a couple of times and then the
>VXT 2000+ continues its booting and comes up.  My question is,
>is this message indicative of a poor configuration or problem?
>I have checked all the manuals that came with both the Infoserver
>and the VXT 2000+.  There is no mention of this error.
>
>Further information on my configuration, my Infoserver disk has
>282720 free blocks and the SERVER EXTENSION LIMIT is set to 40000.
>The largest pagefile on the Infoserver is less than 20000. There
>is 4MB of memory on each VXT 2000+.  The Infoserver software is
>version 2.0 and the VXT 2000+ software is at version 1.2.

Kathy,

	You're running really old software.  The current InfoServer version
is V3.3 and the current VXT version is V2.1.

	There are a couple of possible issues here.  One possibility is
fragmentation of the disk.  SHOW PARTITIONS DKn FULL will give you some
idea of how many deleted partitions there are.  In particular, if the FREE
space is 0, you could be running out of partition slots.  New partition
tables are allocated from FREE space only.

	The only way to compress the disk is do a BACKUP and RETRIEVE (both
local InfoServer commands) to another drive.  Before you do that though, I'd
upgrade to at least V3.2 to be on the safe side.  I'd also make copies of
your CONFIGUATION and 2NDARY_CONFIGURATION partitions to be safe.   It's
fairly easy to blow all your services away if you get the sequence wrong.

	Of course, you could ignore all of that and attach another disk to the
InfoServer and add it to the remote device list.  Then blow away the pagefiles
for some of the VXTs and reboot  the terminals.  They will start using the new
drive instead of the old drive.

	If you have additional questions (I'm an InfoServer engineer, not a
VXT guru) send me mail at the address below.

						--Doug
