In article <45bhmv$bo7@sloth.swcp.com>, phdewi@swcp.com (Phillip Williams) writes:
> I have a bunch of LK250 keyboards with the mini din jacks on them. I was
> whats the difference between a 250 and the 201??

  LK250's are for use with PC's. They're a superset of the XT keyboard
design which also work with AT's. The default [useless] plug on them is
for a VAXmate. It also happens to be the plug that IBM used on the key-
board end of some of their keyboards. You could get them with an alter-
nate cable ending in a regular 5-pin DIN plug. I've never seen an LK250
with a mini-DIN (6 pins), though I've seen LK450's that way.

  The LK201 is a keyboard for a VT2xx/VT3xx terminal and uses a modular
phone plug.

  The 201 and 250 are not interchangeable.

