In article <45cgei$mko@styx.uwa.edu.au>,
Roberto Togneri <roberto@ee.uwa.edu.au> wrote:
>Hi All,
>
>Our department is investigating how to upgrade our current workstations
>environment of delapidated SUN workstations. The main contending vendors
>are SUN, DEC and SGI and we are looking at the following configuration:

It never hurts to replace Suns...  :-)

>server + graphics workstations clients (about 20)
>
>Hopefully this will solve our current problems with distributed SUN
>workstations (if one breaks down everything is affected) by placing all
>services on the server which is properly configured (maximum maintenance,
>disk mirroring, etc.) and keeping the graphics workstations with the same plain
>vanilla system installation (easy to maintain). 
>
>First question: Is this is indeed a good way to go?

Sure, but remember you now have "all your eggs in one basket".  If the server
is critical to your operation, you'll have to think about what you're going
to do if it breaks.  Having disks scattered all over your workstation
farm and making everything depend on everthing else is a system
administrator's nightmare, so go with the server.

>Second question: Does anybody have DEC and SGI workstations on the same network?
>Any problems connecting them together? How about any combination of SUN,DEC
>and SGI, is there a fatal combination we should avoid?

We have DEC Alphas, DECstations (MIPS), SGIs, and Suns all working with
each other.  One of our Alphas is a NIS master with two Suns as slaves.
We're using a couple of Suns (one running Solaris 2, the other running
SunOS) as file servers, with a couple of other systems (Alpha and Sun)
doing some specialized file service.  The SGIs are just along for the
ride.

Another group of systems have several DECstations (one is NIS master
and disk server) with a Sun and an SGI as clients.

One problem you'll have if you have any systems running SunOS 4.1.x is
that Sun decided that if you want to use nameservers you need to use
NIS and have a Sun as the NIS server.  The way around this is to rebuild
the shared C library to have the contents of libresolve.a in it, so that
gethostbyname et al will use the nameservers exclusively for name lookups.
Sun "got it right" in Solaris 2 by allowing you to specify how you want
hostname lookups to be done.  Digital has done it right for several
years, and if I remember correctly SGI does too.


