In article <DG418r.27v@world.std.com>, mbg@world.std.com (Megan) writes:
>>3) Is it worth purchasing?
> 
> If you are looking for it to be something other than a terminal, then
> no.  But if you want a color graphics terminal, sure...

Even as a color graphics terminal it has some problems.  The graphics
characteristics are, well, bizarre.  It has a 768x480 frame buffer
that's just one pixel deep.  To get colors, each horizontal group of
12 pixels share a common set of four "attribute bits" that determine
the color (of eight possible, from a pallette of eight) and blinking
or not.  Thus you can only have 64 different-colored items across a
horizontal line.  Draw a horizontal red line intersecting with a green
line and near the intersection the red line will become green for a
few pixels on either side of the intersection (if the red line is
drawn first, that is). 

A long time ago I wrote a VMS performance display that actually *took 
advantage* of this; the vertical bar graphs were 12 pixels wide and 
were craftily aligned on the attribute-set boundaries, so that the 
color of an entire bar segment could be changed just by drawing a 
vertical line, anywhere within the bar, of the desired color -- the 
"area fill" only had to be done once when the display was initialized. 
But for most purposes it's just a pain.  

