>Hello all, I am interested in finding information about a DEC GIGI
>computer which is about to go up for tender.
>It looks like a cloned BBC micro, except it has RGB and mono outputs,
>as well as several ports on the back. No internal disk, and no sign
>of life when powered up.

The GIGI, also known as a VK100, is a graphics terminal with a built-in
BASIC.  It understands a subset of ReGIS and can drive a monitor using
RGB or composite video.  It has a serial line to connect to a host
machine and if I remember correctly a port for connecting to a
printer.

There is a protocol which allows the internal BASIC to request files
for execution via down-line load from the host and for up-line dumping
to the host.

>So, the big questions are:
>1) What operating system did/does it use?

It doesn't use any operating system.

>2) What/where were these machines originally used?

As graphics displays... I wrote some software a number of years ago to
provide an updating schedule (sort of like what you get on cable tv
nowadays) which used ReGIS on a GIGI...  There were graphics products
from Digital which used GIGIs...

>3) Is it worth purchasing?

If you are looking for it to be something other than a terminal, then
no.  But if you want a color graphics terminal, sure...

