references: <44s2ej$d40@mozo.cc.purdue.edu>
organization: Digital Equipment Corp
reply-to: hoffman@xdelta.enet.dec.com (Stephen Hoffman)
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


In article <44s2ej$d40@mozo.cc.purdue.edu>, rvw@gauss.math.purdue.edu
(Rich Williams) writes:

   <Various text snipped>

:Sales people are recommending some changes to the MV 4200
:at my mothers work.
:What is a VUP?

   VAX Unit of Performance -- it's a set of benchmarks that can be
   used to compare the relative performance of various VAX processors.

:What are the advantages of a MV 3100-XX
:over a MV 4200 (Other than twice the max memory)?

   The VAX 4000 model 200 is rated at about 5 VUPs, 18 TPS, and up to
   64 MB of memory.  I'm not able to locate the VUPs numbers for the
   MicroVAX 3100 series -- as it's not an industry standard benchmark
   and as it's not applicable to Alpha systems, it's falling out of
   favor.  The MicroVAX 3100 model 96 is *significantly* faster than
   the model 200 -- the TPS estimate is 200.

   The MicroVAX 3100 also has full/native SCSI (SCSI-2) capabilities,
   while the VAX 4000 model 200 connects to SCSI via the DSSI.  The
   VAX 4000 model 200 also has a Q-bus, where the MicroVAX 3100 does
   not have a Q-bus.

:How much speed improvement should one expect with the only 
:changes being 3100-85 or 3100-96 over a 4200 same amount 
:of memory and disk?

   I won't hazard a guess, as there's far more than just CPU speed
   involved in determining application performance.  I'd _expect_
   anything from no improvement (application that is disk-bound),
   to ten times (CPU-bound and scales with TPS), and possibly even
   additional improvement given the additional memory supported.
   
:What should you pay for something like a MV 3100-96?

   Call around and ask.  (I have no idea what the street prices for
   these systems are.)  Given your comments and the processor models
   involved, this appears to be a purchase, and not an upgrade.
   (Most upgrades from the VAX 4000 model 200 go to the VAX 4000
   model 106A -- the model 106A can also hook to and [re]use the
   model 200's Q-bus enclosure, and this can be potentially useful
   if there are any "odd" Q-bus devices required or in use.)

   Do *not* skimp on physical memory -- an under-configured memory
   system can have a major impact on overall performance.

