From: wilsonj@alum01.its.rpi.edu (John Wilson)
Subject: Re: RX-50 diskettes on pc?
Date: 1995/10/02
Message-ID: <44p7ep$1df8@alum01.its.rpi.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 116958944
references: <44ob1s$ktn$1@mhafm.production.compuserve.com>
organization: Rensselaer Polytechnic Institute
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec,comp.sys.dec.micro

In article <44ob1s$ktn$1@mhafm.production.compuserve.com>,
Thomas Hoeper  <100350.612@CompuServe.COM> wrote:
>In the company we have a 10 years old Micro PDP-11 that is
>used only for one purpose: creating a simple data file on RX-50 
>diskette
>about three times a year used in a remote site.
>
>Does anyone know if this is possible?

Sure...  You didn't say what OS you were running on the 11 though.
The RT-11 disk layout is simple and is supported by the RT11.EXE
program, and also by my PUTR.COM, which comes with source code
(on tats.wizvax.net, pub/putr).  It's also supported by system utilities
in RSX and RSTS and UNIX, so you're most likely covered on the 11 end.

>In this case the least solution I can think of is:
>We would need a program that reads the blocks out of a
>existing RX-50 data diskette into a pc file. We then could patch
>the data there and write back the blocks to a RX-50 diskette.

If you want to go that route (like if your disks have no file system but
are just raw blocks), you may want to grab my FDCDEMO program (from
tats.wizvax.net, pub/ibmpc/floppy), which also comes with sources.
It contains some fairly flexible floppy read/write/format code, along
with a simple command interpreter to demo it, which among other things
has commands to read and write entire disks to/from image files.  RX50s
are specifically supported (as are any other kind of FM/MFM disk that has
constant-sized sectors, FM requires the right FDC though).  Note that
untangling the soft interleave is not done by this program, your program
to massage the data would have to figure this out (the standard interleave
layout for RX50s is described in comments in PUTR.ASM).

John Wilson



