X-Deja-AN: 116958955
distribution: world
references: <44p75o$fge@ipgate.le.ac.uk>
organization: Digital Equipment Corp
reply-to: hoffman@xdelta.enet.dec.com (Stephen Hoffman)
newsgroups: comp.periphs.scsi,comp.sys.dec,comp.os.vms


In article <44p75o$fge@ipgate.le.ac.uk>, Jeremy Ashley <jka> writes:

:I've been offered a SCSI drive (RZ23) from an old VAX work station.  It's an
:internal drive and plugged straight into my adaptect SCSI card without any
:problems.  On rebooting and running the cards disk utilities, the disk was
:recognised for what it was, but when I tried to format the disk using the card,
:I received:
:  SCSI command failure <blah>
:  Sense key - device not ready <blah>

  You might want to tell us what <blah> is.

:I seem to recall hearing somewhere that DEC VAX SCSI devices are not really
:using SCSI protocols, and have been lobotomised to their own proprietry
:specifications.  

  You recall (or were told) wrong.  Digital's SCSI storage devices are
  as standard as any other SCSI device of a similar vintage -- SCSI is
  a moving target, and older versions of SCSI were little more than
  collections of standardized and optionally-implemented features.
  (Recent iterations of the SCSI standards are in far better shape...)
  The Digital SCSI devices comply with the SCSI standards they were
  designed to meet.  Settings for various SCSI features -- such as the
  spin-up-on-power-up setting -- for RZ-series devices are tailored and
  tested for use with Digital operating systems.)

  VAXstation 2000 and MicroVAX 2000 series systems used a bus that was
  similar to the SCSI.

:My question (finally!).  Is it worth persuing this, can this
:drive ever be made to work with a standard SCSI card, by software or hardware
:means (ie, soldering iron, wirecutters etc) or .... not?

  There are Digital systems using one of the Adaptec SCSI controllers.

:oh yes... and is this enough information to make an informative guess?   Or
:does it all depend upon numbers and dates and revisions.

  You might want to tell us what the host system is, and which Adaptec
  controller is in use, and the controller BIOS revision.   (I'm guessing
  this is a PC running Microsoft Windows or DOS.)

  Drivers for Microsoft DOS and Windows operating systems generally
  require that the disk devices spin up automatically on power-up,
  where larger systems generally ask the disk to spin up -- staggered
  startups can be used to avoid overloading the system's power supply.
  If memory serves, NT prefers to spin up the drives.)

  There is a program around -- I believe it is available via ftp
  from Digital's gatekeeper site -- called RZSPINUP -- that can be
  used to toggle the status of the spin-up-on-power-up setting.
  (Recent Adaptec 1540C controllers can apparently optionally
  perform the drive spinup from within the controller.)
  
  You will also need termination if you don't already have it -- the
  Digital part number for a terminator is/was 13-26547-01.

  You may also end up needing FDISK and FORMAT, and if that fails,
  SCSIFMT and then FDISK and FORMAT again.  You'll also need to make
  sure you have the appropriate drivers loaded.

  ------------------------------ Opinionative -------------------------------
   Stephen Hoffman      OpenVMS Engineering      hoffman@xdelta.enet.dec.com
